<content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>Earlier, students encountered the idea of equivalent systems. They described the moves that lead to equivalent equations and explained why the solution to those equations was the same as the solution to the original system. This activity reinforces that work.</para>
<para>Given an unordered set of equivalent systems, students work with a partner to arrange the systems such that the order constitutes logical steps toward solving an original system. Along the way, they take turns describing how each system came from the previous one and explaining how they know it has the same solution as its predecessor. The work allows students to practice constructing logical arguments and critiquing those of others.</para>
<para>Here is an image of the cards for reference and planning.</para>
<para><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/91ab017eb03d410c7913332368447589cc19517b"><media><image alt="Sample image of card sheet used for activity." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4839ff4fed388fea8259fd3a237d649c4f8ad71e"/></media></link></para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Arrange students in groups of 2. Give one set of pre-cut slips or cards from the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cf2897b7c4dc94243da9c230507e919108f984bc">blackline master</link> to each group. Ask students to find the slip or card that shows a system of equations and is labeled "Start here."</para>
<para>Explain that all the other slips contain equivalent systems that represent steps in solving the starting system. Ask students to arrange the slips in the order that would lead to its solution, and to make sure they can explain what moves take each system to the next system and why each system is equivalent to the one before it.</para>
<para>Partners should take turns finding the next step in the solving process and explaining their reasoning. As one student explains, the partner's job is to listen and make sure they agree and the explanation makes sense. If they disagree, the partners should discuss until they reach an agreement.</para>
<para>Give students 5 minutes to arrange the systems. Follow with a whole-class discussion.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Your teacher will give you some slips of paper with systems of equations written on them. Each system represents a step in solving this system:</para>
<para>Arrange the slips in the order that would lead to a solution. Be prepared to:</para>

<!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!--Q#-->

      <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
          <item>Describe what move takes one system to the next system.</item>
      </list>     
      <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
      <para>\( \begin {cases}\frac45 x + 6y = 15\\ \text-x + 18y = 11 \end{cases} \)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis></para>
      <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}4x + 30y = 75\\ \text-x + 18y = 11 \end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis></para>
      <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}4x + 30y = 75\\ \text-4x + 72y = 44 \end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis></para>
      <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}4x + 30y &amp;= \hspace{2mm}75\\ 102y &amp;=119 \end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>     
      <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis></para>
      <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}4x + 30y &amp;=75\\ y &amp;=\frac76 \end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>      
      <para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis></para>
      <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}4x +35 &amp;=75\\ y &amp;=\frac76 \end{align}\end{cases} \)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 6 - </emphasis></para>
      <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}4x &amp;=40\\ y &amp;=\frac76\end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 7 - </emphasis></para>
      <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}x &amp;=10\\ y &amp;=\frac76\end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>  

  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
     <item>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the original system and step 1.
      <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
     </item>
     <item>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the system in step 1 to the system in step 2.
      <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
     </item>
     <item>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the system in step 2 to the system in step 3.
      <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
     </item>
     <item>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the system in step 3 to the system in step 4.
      <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
     </item>
     <item>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the system in step 4 to the system in step 5.
      <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
     </item>
     <item>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the system in step 5 to the system in step 6.
      <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
     </item>
     <item>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the system in step 6 to the system in step 7.
      <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
     </item>
</list>     
<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<title>Student Facing Extension</title>
<title>Are you ready for more?</title>
<para>This system of equations has the solution \( (5,\text-2) \): \( \begin {cases}Ax - By = 24\\ Bx + Ay = 31 \end{cases} \)</para>
<para>1. Find the missing value of \( A \).</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( A = 2 \)</para>
<para>2. Find the missing value of \( B \).</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( B = 7 \)</para>


<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>Students who are thinking algorithmically about solving systems of equations may think that the first step should involve multiplying each side of the second equation by \( \frac{4}{5} \), becoming frustrated when no cards show that step. Encourage them to compare both the first and the second equations in the starting card to the first and second equations on other cards to gain some ideas about what steps might have been taken.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis
</title>
<para>Ask students to share the order in which the systems should be arranged to lead to the solution. Display the ordered systems for all to see.</para>
<para>Point out to students that this particular solution path involves multiplying each of the two equations by a factor in order to eliminate the \( x \)-variable. Ask students if it's possible to eliminate a variable by multiplying only one equation by a factor. (Yes, we could multiply the first equation by \( \frac{5}{4} \) to eliminate \( x \), or by 3 to eliminate y. Or we could multiply the second equation by \( \frac{4}{5} \) to eliminate \( x \), or by \( \frac{1}{3} \) to eliminate \( y \).)</para>


<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para><title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para><title>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Speaking, Representing</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">

    <para>Use this routine to support student conversation as they take turns selecting cards and explaining how each system came from the previous one. Display the following prompts for students to use as they respond to reasoning shared by the partner who selected the card: “I agree because…” or “I disagree because…” Encourage students not only to challenge each other if they disagree with the card selected, but also to press for clear explanations that use mathematical language.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Design Principle(s): Support sense-making</para>
  </div></div>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para><title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title></para>
  <para><title>Action and Expression: Internalize Executive Functions</title></para>
   </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>Chunk this task into more manageable parts to support students who benefit from support with organization and problem solving. For example, guide students in a strategy by prompting them to begin by identifying the first and last cards. Next, ask students to identify the card where the solution for y first appears, and to explain how they know. Encourage students to use these three cards as benchmarks for sorting the remaining cards.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Supports accessibility for: Organization; Attention</para>
  </div>
</div>
  
<!--END ELL AND SWD GRAY BOX -->

<title/>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their
  understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Solve the system of equations using elimination.</para>
<table>
<tgroup cols="2"><thead>
  <row>
    <entry>Answers</entry>
    <entry>Feedback</entry>
  </row>
</thead>
<tbody>
  <row>
    <entry>(7, –5)</entry>
    <entry>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Does the ordered pair make both equations true? You can multiply the first equation by 5 and the second equation by 2. The correct answer is \( ( \frac{1}{2} , \frac{1}{4}) \)  </entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>(1, 2)</entry>
    <entry>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Does the
        ordered pair make both equations true? You can multiply the first equation by 5
        and the second equation by 2. The correct answer is \( ( \frac{1}{2} , \frac{1}{4}) \)  </entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>(0, –1.5)</entry>
    <entry>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Does the
        ordered pair make both equations true? You can multiply the first equation by 5
        and the second equation by 2. The correct answer is \( ( \frac{1}{2} , \frac{1}{4}) \)  </entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>\( ( \frac{1}{2} , \frac{1}{4}) \)</entry>
    <entry>That’s correct! Check yourself: The ordered pair makes
        both equations true.</entry>
  </row>
</tbody>
</tgroup></table><title>2.4.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it. </emphasis></emphasis></para>
<title>Solving a System of Equations by Elimination Involving Fractions</title>
<para>You just ordered the steps for solving a system of equations using elimination. Try
    using these steps when the system has equations containing fractions.</para>
<para>When the system of equations contains fractions, we will first clear the fractions by
    multiplying each equation by the least common denominator (LCD) of all the
    fractions in the equation.</para>
<para>Solve the system by elimination: \( \left\{ \begin{array}{c l} x + \frac{1}{2}y =6\\ \frac{3}{2}x + \frac{2}{3}y = \frac{17}{2} \end{array}\right. \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Solution:</emphasis></para>

  <!--BEGIN STEPS-->
	<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Check to see if each equation is in standard form \(Ax+By=C\).<emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>To clear the fractions, multiply each equation by its LCD (Least Common Denominator).<emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Step 3 -  Simplify the equations.<emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Eliminate one of the variables and simplify the equation.<emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Add the two equations to eliminate \(y\). <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS WITH LINE-->
	  \(\begin{align*}-8x-4y&amp;=-48
	  \\9x+4y&amp;=51
	  \\ \hline
	   x&amp;=3
	  \end{align*}\)<!--End ALIGN TO EQUALS WITH LINE-->	
	<emphasis>Step 6 - </emphasis>Solve for \(y\) by substituting \(x\) back into one of the equations.<!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS -->
	\(\begin{align*}2x+y&amp;=12
	\\2(3)+y&amp;=12
	\\6+y&amp;=12
	\\y&amp;=6
	\\\end{align*}\)<!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->		
	<emphasis>Step 7 - </emphasis>Write the solution as an ordered pair.<emphasis>Step 8 - </emphasis>Check that the ordered pair is a solution to <emphasis>both</emphasis> original equations.<!--BEGIN SIDE BY SIDE EQUATIONS-->
	</para><para>\(\begin{array}{rr}
      &amp; \frac32 x+\frac23 y= \frac{17}{2}	\\
    x+\frac12 y \overset?=6 &amp; \frac32 (3)+\frac23 (6) \overset?= \frac{17}{2}\\
    3+\frac12 (6) \overset?= 6&amp;\frac92 +4 \overset?=\frac{17}{2}	\\
    3+3\overset?=6 &amp; \frac92 + \frac82 \overset?=\frac{17}{2} \\  
    6=6\checkmark &amp; \frac{17}{2}=\frac{17}{2}\checkmark
    \end{array}\) </para>
    <!--END SIDE BY SIDE EQUATIONS -->
   
   <para>The solution is \((3,6)\).</para>
<!-- END STEPS -->

<title>Try It: Solving a System of Equations by Elimination Involving Fractions</title>
<para>Solve the following system of equations.</para>
<para>\( \left\{ \begin{array}{c l} \frac{1}{3}x - \frac{1}{2}y =1\\ \frac{3}{4}x - y = \frac{5}{2} \end{array}\right. \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>
</para><para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Check to see if each equation is in standard form \(Ax+By=C\).<emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>To clear the fractions, multiply each equation by its LCD (Least Common Denominator).<emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Step 3 -  Simplify the equations.<emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>To get equivalent coefficients of \(x\), we will need to multiply the first equation by 3 and the second equation by 2.<emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Simplify. <emphasis>Step 6 - </emphasis>Subtract the equations to eliminate one variable. <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS WITH LINE-->		
  \(\begin{align*}6x-9y&amp;=18
  \\6x-8y&amp;=20
  \\ \hline
  -y&amp;=-2
  \\y&amp;=2
  \end{align*}\)<!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS WITH LINE-->	
  <emphasis>Step 7 - </emphasis>Solve for \(x\) by substituting \(y\) back into one of the equations.<!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS -->	
  \(\begin{align*}2x-3y&amp;=6
  \\2x-3(2)&amp;=6
  \\2x-6&amp;=6
  \\2x&amp;=12
  \\x&amp;=6
  \\\end{align*}\)<!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->		
  <emphasis>Step 8 - </emphasis>Write the solution as an ordered pair.<emphasis>Step 9 - </emphasis>Check that the ordered pair is a solution to <emphasis>both</emphasis> original equations.<!--BEGIN SIDE BY SIDE EQUATIONS-->
  </para><para>\(\begin{array}{rr}
    &amp; \frac34 -y = \frac{5}{2}	\\
      &amp; \frac34 (6)-2= \frac52	\\
    \frac13 x-\frac12 \overset?=1 &amp; \frac{18}{4}-2 \overset?= \frac52 \\
    \frac13 (6)\frac12 (6) \overset?= 6&amp;\frac{18}{4} -\frac84 \overset?=\frac52	\\
    2-1\overset?=1 &amp; \frac92 + \frac{10}{4} \overset?= \frac52 \\  
    1=1\checkmark &amp; \frac52=\frac52 \checkmark
    \end{array}\) </para>
    <!--END SIDE BY SIDE EQUATIONS -->
<para>
The solution is \((6,2)\).</para>
<!-- END STEPS -->
 
</section>
</content>
